---
import DiagramLabel from './DiagramLabel.astro'

// Make graph RAG earn its cost. Split the query set into flat lookups and genuinely relational,
// multi hop questions, run both strategies over both slices, and adopt the graph only where it wins.
// The DISCIPLINE is the lesson here: adopt a retriever because a study came back yes at a fair
// budget on the queries that need it, not because graphs are fashionable.
//
// EVERY NUMBER IN THIS FIGURE IS ILLUSTRATIVE. An earlier draft ended "Numbers are the ones the
// Atlas relational split reports". There is no such split, and no measurement was ever taken behind
// these figures. Atlas ships NEITHER retriever this figure compares: there is no vector index and no
// graph retriever in the tree. Confirm with:
//   grep -rn "rerank\|vector\|embedding\|neo4j" --include="*.py" agent evals corpus_spine harness
// The only hit is an embedding column in the chunk parquet that evals/measure_retrieval.py documents
// as present and unused, because scoring a query against it would need the same embedding model on
// the query side and no offline run has one. What Atlas actually ships is a lexical coverage
// fraction (agent/domain/retrieval.py, lexical_score); its measured behaviour is in GatingRule.

const SLICES = {
  flat: {
    label: 'Flat lookups',
    q: '“what is the fair use threshold?”',
    vector: 1.0,
    graph: 1.0,
    verdict: 'skip the graph',
    win: false,
    why: 'the vector retriever is already perfect; the graph adds only cost.',
  },
  relational: {
    label: 'Relational / multi hop',
    q: '“will the account holder’s line be throttled?”',
    vector: 0.5,
    graph: 1.0,
    verdict: 'adopt the graph',
    win: true,
    why: 'the link the answer needs is a graph edge, not co-occurring text, so no lookup finds it at any k; the traversal resolves the customer and collects every hop.',
  },
}
const pct = (x: number) => `${(x * 100).toFixed(0)}%`
---

{/* Statically rendered on the relational slice, where the graph earns its cost and the illustrative
    0.50 against 1.00 split makes the point, so a no-JS render never
    implies "adopt the graph" without the slice it actually wins on. The script
    flips the interactive view to the flat slice on init, where the graph adds
    only cost, so the "prove it per slice" lesson is intact. */}
<figure class="not-prose my-10 border border-border-dark bg-surface rounded-lg p-5" data-gv data-win="true" data-inview>
  <div class="mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
    <DiagramLabel label="Make graph RAG earn its cost, per slice" />
    <div class="h-px flex-1 bg-border-dark"></div>
  </div>

  <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 mb-5" role="group" aria-label="Which slice of the query set">
    <span class="font-mono text-mono-xs text-text-muted mr-1" aria-hidden="true">Query slice:</span>
    <button type="button" class="gv-toggle inline-flex items-center font-mono text-mono-xs px-3 min-h-11 border border-border-dark text-text-muted transition-colors" data-gv-mode="flat" aria-pressed="false">
      flat lookups
    </button>
    <button type="button" class="gv-toggle on inline-flex items-center font-mono text-mono-xs px-3 min-h-11 border border-border-dark transition-colors" data-gv-mode="relational" aria-pressed="true">
      relational
    </button>
  </div>

  <p class="m-0 mb-4 font-mono text-mono-xs text-text-muted" data-gv-q>{SLICES.relational.q}</p>

  <div class="space-y-4" aria-hidden="true">
    <div>
      <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between mb-1.5 font-mono text-mono-xs">
        <span class="text-text-primary">Vector recall@3</span>
        <span class="text-text-muted" data-gv-vec-label>{SLICES.relational.vector.toFixed(2)}</span>
      </div>
      <div class="gv-track relative h-3 w-full">
        <div class="gv-fill gv-vec absolute inset-y-0 left-0" data-gv-vec style={`width:${pct(SLICES.relational.vector)}`}></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between mb-1.5 font-mono text-mono-xs">
        <span class="text-text-primary">Graph path recall</span>
        <span class="text-text-muted" data-gv-graph-label>{SLICES.relational.graph.toFixed(2)}</span>
      </div>
      <div class="gv-track relative h-3 w-full">
        <div class="gv-fill gv-graph absolute inset-y-0 left-0" data-gv-graph style={`width:${pct(SLICES.relational.graph)}`}></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-5 flex items-center gap-2">
    <span class="gv-verdict font-mono text-mono-xs uppercase tracking-wider px-2 py-1 border" data-gv-verdict>{SLICES.relational.verdict}</span>
  </div>

  <figcaption class="m-0 mt-4 border-t border-border-dark pt-4 font-mono text-mono-xs text-text-muted" data-gv-caption aria-live="polite">
    On the relational slice the split is stark: the vector recovers only 0.50 even at the deployed budget (k=3), while graph path recall is 1.00, every hop. {SLICES.relational.why} Adopt the graph here. Worked example, not an Atlas measurement: Atlas ships neither a vector index nor a graph retriever, so the shape of the decision is the transferable part, not these figures.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<style is:global>
  [data-gv] .gv-toggle.on {
    border-color: var(--color-text-primary);
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-text-primary) 6%, transparent);
  }
  [data-gv] .gv-track {
    background: var(--color-surface-container-high);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border-dark);
    border-radius: var(--radius-full);
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  [data-gv] .gv-fill {
    border-radius: var(--radius-full);
    transition: width 0.45s var(--ease-out-quint);
  }
  [data-gv] .gv-vec {
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-text-muted) 45%, transparent);
  }
  [data-gv] .gv-graph {
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-primary) 60%, transparent);
  }
  [data-gv] .gv-verdict {
    border-color: var(--color-border-dark);
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
  }
  [data-gv][data-win='true'] .gv-verdict {
    border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-primary) 50%, transparent);
    color: var(--color-primary);
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    [data-gv] .gv-fill {
      transition: none;
    }
  }
</style>
